1
00:00:26,879 --> 00:00:52,620
Since the turn of the century, the United States has undergone a phenomenal growth in population and industry.

2
00:00:53,140 --> 00:00:58,379
The availability of economical electrical power has been a major influence on this growth.

3
00:00:58,880 --> 00:01:10,480
Our requirements have more than doubled every decade for the past 40 years, and even greater

4
00:01:10,480 --> 00:01:12,240
demand is predicted in the future.

5
00:01:17,640 --> 00:01:22,520
The growth and development of practically every nation in the world is likewise being

6
00:01:22,520 --> 00:01:26,099
influenced by the availability of economical electrical energy.

7
00:01:28,879 --> 00:01:35,799
Simultaneously, fossil fuels are becoming an important source of raw materials for the

8
00:01:35,799 --> 00:01:46,199
manufacture of new and valuable products.

9
00:01:46,199 --> 00:01:52,919
The projected lifetime of normal fuel sources is therefore rapidly diminishing despite exploration

10
00:01:52,919 --> 00:01:58,280
and improved techniques of recovery, and the cost of generating electrical energy is expected

11
00:01:58,280 --> 00:02:01,960
to increase.

12
00:02:01,960 --> 00:02:06,980
The United States Atomic Energy Commission is developing nuclear energy as a source of

13
00:02:06,980 --> 00:02:13,599
economical power to meet increasing power demand throughout the world.

14
00:02:36,979 --> 00:02:57,979
The AEC's Power Demonstration Program

15
00:02:57,979 --> 00:03:03,479
is aimed at demonstrating the potential of various types of nuclear power reactor systems

16
00:03:03,479 --> 00:03:06,019
for central station power applications.

17
00:03:06,979 --> 00:03:10,840
Data from the design, construction, and operation of these plants

18
00:03:10,840 --> 00:03:18,139
are used for the design of subsequent plants in keeping with the Commission's goals for developing economic nuclear power.

19
00:03:37,960 --> 00:03:46,400
The commission's reactor development program also provides for the development of advanced concepts requiring a higher degree of technical refinement.

20
00:03:48,660 --> 00:03:55,280
One of the various concepts being developed, the natural uranium-fueled heavy water moderated reactor,

21
00:03:55,780 --> 00:04:03,800
has potential for achieving economical power because of the relatively low cost and greater availability of natural uranium as a fuel,

22
00:04:03,800 --> 00:04:07,040
and the characteristics of deuterium as a moderator.

23
00:04:07,640 --> 00:04:12,040
Such a plant would be independent of costly facilities for the enrichment of uranium.

24
00:04:14,439 --> 00:04:18,819
The availability and cost of heavy water to moderate such a reactor

25
00:04:18,819 --> 00:04:21,680
had always been a problem in development of this concept.

26
00:04:33,800 --> 00:04:44,460
In 1952, a new AEC installation, the Savannah River plant, became operational.

27
00:04:45,540 --> 00:04:51,300
Located near Aiken, South Carolina, the plant would be operated by the EI DuPont Dinamoors Company

28
00:04:51,300 --> 00:04:56,319
under a prime contract administered by the Savannah River Operations Office of the AEC.

29
00:04:56,319 --> 00:05:07,740
The AEC's Savannah River plant, built to produce plutonium and other vital nuclear materials, includes a heavy water extraction facility.

30
00:05:10,420 --> 00:05:18,139
Production of deuterium in the form of heavy water, or D2O, from this plant, at one time more than 1,000 tons a year,

31
00:05:18,819 --> 00:05:23,939
provided the moderator for Savannah River production reactors and other requirements of the AEC,

32
00:05:24,300 --> 00:05:26,899
including both foreign and domestic sales.

33
00:05:27,819 --> 00:05:32,540
Heavy water costs have been reduced from $70 a pound to $28 a pound.

34
00:05:36,920 --> 00:05:40,699
A research and development facility, the Savannah River Laboratory,

35
00:05:41,040 --> 00:05:47,379
was established to perform diversified investigations with emphasis on the technology of heavy water reactors.

36
00:05:53,939 --> 00:06:14,279
In November 1956, DuPont was requested to extend its activities

37
00:06:14,279 --> 00:06:19,000
to include the investigation of natural uranium heavy water power reactors.

38
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:31,759
A study of reactor design concepts in the 100 electrical megawatt range was initiated.

39
00:06:31,759 --> 00:06:35,980
This was the minimum size that was believed to be economically attractive for development

40
00:06:35,980 --> 00:06:39,319
in the United States.

41
00:06:39,319 --> 00:06:44,319
Early investigations, however, indicated that none of the concepts studied in the 100 electrical

42
00:06:44,319 --> 00:06:49,719
megawatt range, could compete with fossil fuels, even in high power cost areas of

43
00:06:49,719 --> 00:06:57,579
the country. A continuation of the study with design investigations of 200, 300

44
00:06:57,579 --> 00:07:02,139
and 400 electrical megawatts was performed.

45
00:07:02,680 --> 00:07:07,279
Results indicated that a sizable reduction in power cost was possible in

46
00:07:07,279 --> 00:07:11,920
larger size plants. Additional development of reactor components and

47
00:07:11,920 --> 00:07:16,640
fuels was required to reduce capital and operating costs to competitive levels.

48
00:07:19,759 --> 00:07:24,560
To carry out this program, it was determined that a facility would be necessary for

49
00:07:24,560 --> 00:07:31,200
full-scale testing of fuels and reactor components. A conceptual design of a test reactor was developed.

50
00:07:31,199 --> 00:07:45,920
In November 1958, authority was granted to proceed with the design and construction of

51
00:07:45,920 --> 00:07:53,259
a test reactor to be known as the Heavy Water Components Test Reactor, or HWCTR, and located

52
00:07:53,259 --> 00:08:04,339
at the Savannah River plant.

53
00:08:04,339 --> 00:08:22,420
The site chosen for the HWCTR was easily accessible and could use existing supporting facilities.

54
00:08:22,420 --> 00:08:28,340
was initiated ground was broken and plans advanced for construction of the hw ctr

55
00:08:38,580 --> 00:08:44,100
in addition to the test reactor project the aec's savannah river operations office was

56
00:08:44,100 --> 00:08:49,539
assigned responsibility for development of natural uranium heavy water power reactors concept

57
00:08:49,539 --> 00:08:55,799
As a result of studies, it was determined that a pressure vessel liquid-cooled reactor

58
00:08:55,799 --> 00:08:58,659
could probably be built with existing technology.

59
00:08:59,740 --> 00:09:04,659
However, a more advanced design, a pressure tube boiling direct cycle reactor,

60
00:09:05,259 --> 00:09:09,879
indicated lower power costs and the greatest promise of being economically competitive

61
00:09:09,879 --> 00:09:11,679
with conventional power plants.

62
00:09:15,139 --> 00:09:19,019
Five major areas required additional research and development.

63
00:09:19,539 --> 00:09:24,659
to confirm feasibility prior to constructing a prototype heavy water power reactor.

64
00:09:27,219 --> 00:09:32,500
Lattice studies to determine optimum fuel lifetime and studies of safety and stability.

65
00:09:35,539 --> 00:09:40,099
Fuel element selection and development of inexpensive fabrication techniques.

66
00:09:40,100 --> 00:09:50,519
studies of heavy water heat transfer chemistry hydraulics and leakage problems especially vapor

67
00:09:50,519 --> 00:09:58,220
losses at high temperatures and pressures selection and evaluation of special components

68
00:09:58,220 --> 00:10:05,800
for heavy water reactor service the possible use of conventional materials and components

69
00:10:05,800 --> 00:10:07,720
for lowering capital costs.

70
00:10:14,840 --> 00:10:18,400
A program to study the physics of heavy water lattices

71
00:10:18,400 --> 00:10:22,060
was initiated to determine optimum design parameters.

72
00:10:24,320 --> 00:10:29,980
The process development pile, a large critical facility of the Savannah River Laboratory,

73
00:10:29,980 --> 00:10:32,560
was modified to permit these studies.

74
00:10:35,800 --> 00:10:40,260
The large stainless steel tank holding over 100 tons of heavy water

75
00:10:40,960 --> 00:10:43,960
permitted studies in a broad range of lattice arrangements.

76
00:10:44,320 --> 00:10:48,800
Both uranium metal and uranium oxide fuel materials were investigated.

77
00:10:56,640 --> 00:11:03,780
Special facilities permitted subcritical screening studies and extension of data to conditions of elevated temperatures and pressures.

78
00:11:05,800 --> 00:11:13,880
Data from these experiments was analyzed on high-speed computers.

79
00:11:14,160 --> 00:11:20,460
The performance and safety characteristics of various conceptual designs were also analyzed and predicted.

80
00:11:28,260 --> 00:11:33,940
The fuel development program included a systematic search for low-cost methods of fabrication,

81
00:11:33,940 --> 00:11:43,900
A process of co-extruding tubular fuel elements of uranium metal with a zirconium or zircaloy cladding was developed at Nuclear Metals Incorporated.

82
00:11:47,280 --> 00:11:52,940
Proper design of the extrusion billet permitted variation of core and cladding dimensions.

83
00:11:53,960 --> 00:12:01,980
Fuel tubes with core thicknesses of 130 to 250 mils and lengths of 10 feet were produced for irradiation tests.

84
00:12:04,540 --> 00:12:11,000
Driver fuel and the metallic uranium test fuel for HWCTR was produced by this process.

85
00:12:16,120 --> 00:12:20,800
Alloys of uranium, such as uranium zirconium and uranium molybdenum,

86
00:12:21,020 --> 00:12:26,540
were studied to improve dimensional stability under irradiation and increase corrosion resistance.

87
00:12:28,280 --> 00:12:32,680
Samples of these fuel elements were irradiated in the Savannah River production reactors,

88
00:12:32,680 --> 00:12:38,740
the Vallecitos boiling water reactor, and the E-20 loop of the NRU at Chalk River, Canada.

89
00:12:47,920 --> 00:12:53,160
Post-irradiation examination of fuels in high-level caves was performed at Savannah River

90
00:12:53,160 --> 00:12:55,900
to guide development of the fabrication process.

91
00:13:02,680 --> 00:13:21,280
Because of the inherent corrosion resistance and improved stability of uranium oxide, techniques

92
00:13:21,279 --> 00:13:25,639
for the inexpensive manufacture of UO2 fuels were also investigated.

93
00:13:34,240 --> 00:13:39,779
Methods of assembling individual compacted pellets were well established, but were relatively

94
00:13:39,779 --> 00:13:40,299
costly.

95
00:13:42,879 --> 00:13:50,120
Long rods of UO2 compacted by mechanical vibration and swaging of the zircaloy sheathing had

96
00:13:50,120 --> 00:13:55,039
been produced in connection with other programs, such as fuel elements for the plutonium recycle

97
00:13:55,039 --> 00:14:05,259
test reactor.

98
00:14:05,259 --> 00:14:10,440
Work was begun at Savannah River Laboratory to produce a tubular fuel element of vibratory

99
00:14:10,440 --> 00:14:13,919
and swage compacted UO2.

100
00:14:13,919 --> 00:14:19,539
This process, combined with tubular configuration, showed promise of reducing the number of fuel

101
00:14:19,539 --> 00:14:24,699
and operations required for an element thus reducing cost by simplifying

102
00:14:24,699 --> 00:14:33,159
processing and assembly under this fuel development program candidate fuel

103
00:14:33,159 --> 00:14:37,419
elements of both uranium metal and uranium oxide were manufactured for

104
00:14:37,419 --> 00:14:41,339
testing in the HWC TR

105
00:14:49,539 --> 00:14:57,839
In addition to the metallurgical and physics studies, investigations were conducted concurrently at the Savannah River Laboratory's hydraulic test facility

106
00:14:57,839 --> 00:15:02,639
to determine the leakage from various components of the heavy water circulating system.

107
00:15:03,620 --> 00:15:07,919
Specifications for components of the HWCTR were developed in this program.

108
00:15:19,539 --> 00:15:27,459
Data were also obtained on the corrosion and vibration of fuel assemblies exposed to simulated power reactor coolant conditions.

109
00:15:35,620 --> 00:15:43,639
The experimental heat transfer loop systems at Savannah River Laboratory and at Columbia University provided needed engineering data.

110
00:15:44,519 --> 00:15:49,740
Included were studies of fuel burnout and hydraulic stability of a boiling water reactor.

111
00:15:50,799 --> 00:15:56,759
Further development in all these areas would be extended by construction and operation of the HWCTR.

112
00:16:01,600 --> 00:16:08,460
Subgrade structure for the HWCTR required removal of approximately 50,000 cubic yards of earth.

113
00:16:09,419 --> 00:16:13,519
The excavation measured approximately 200 by 200 feet at the surface,

114
00:16:13,639 --> 00:16:15,259
and was 60 feet deep.

115
00:16:17,980 --> 00:16:21,759
A monolithic slab of concrete was placed as the foundation.

116
00:16:32,179 --> 00:16:37,059
The internal concrete structure consisting of the reactor biological shield

117
00:16:37,059 --> 00:16:39,460
and spent fuel basin was erected next.

118
00:16:39,460 --> 00:16:47,259
The reactor building used a new concept for vapor containment design and construction.

119
00:16:47,259 --> 00:16:55,560
Reinforced concrete was used below grade attached to a carbon steel shell above grade. Its overall

120
00:16:55,560 --> 00:17:02,860
dimensions are 70 feet in diameter and 120 feet in height. Tensioned steel strands are used to

121
00:17:02,860 --> 00:17:09,220
strengthen the concrete shell against internal pressure. The 18-inch thick concrete shell was

122
00:17:09,220 --> 00:17:25,220
placed in one continuous pour. After sealing the outside of the concrete wall and backfilling,

123
00:17:25,220 --> 00:17:36,420
the 65 foot high dome portion was bolted to the concrete at grade level.

124
00:17:39,640 --> 00:17:43,539
The building was structurally tested at 29 PSIG

125
00:17:43,539 --> 00:17:47,839
and leak tested at its design pressure of 24 PSIG.

126
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:53,079
A leak rate of only 1% of the volume per 24 hours was specified.

127
00:17:53,079 --> 00:18:00,179
following satisfactory testing a large section was removed from the steel dome to permit

128
00:18:00,179 --> 00:18:09,779
installation of major reactor components the design of the hwctr called for highly specialized

129
00:18:09,779 --> 00:18:16,119
equipment and components for example pumps of special design were built and tested at the

130
00:18:16,119 --> 00:18:22,079
bingham pump company in portland oregon the shaft seals of these pumps would test their

131
00:18:22,079 --> 00:18:40,460
suitability for heavy water power reactor service.

132
00:18:40,460 --> 00:18:46,079
The reactor vessel was manufactured by the Pacific Coast Engineering Company in San Francisco,

133
00:18:46,079 --> 00:18:47,079
California.

134
00:18:47,079 --> 00:18:52,000
The overall height is about 30 feet, with a diameter at the core section of approximately

135
00:18:52,000 --> 00:19:00,720
seven feet. It was fabricated from carbon steel plates and forgings three and a half inches to

136
00:19:00,720 --> 00:19:07,759
five inches thick and weighs about 98 tons. All inside surfaces are clad with a one-quarter inch

137
00:19:07,759 --> 00:19:15,839
layer of type 304 stainless steel. The vessel, manufactured in accordance with the unfired

138
00:19:15,839 --> 00:19:21,119
pressure vessel code of the American Society of Mechanical Engineers, is designed to withstand

139
00:19:21,120 --> 00:19:24,560
and the pressures of 1500 pounds per square inch

140
00:19:24,560 --> 00:19:27,280
at a temperature of 600 degrees fahrenheit.

141
00:19:30,020 --> 00:19:32,320
The vessel was shipped to the site by rail.

142
00:19:40,640 --> 00:19:44,700
It was installed in the reactor building through the large construction opening.

143
00:19:51,120 --> 00:20:08,780
The reactor biological shield has a minimum of 11 feet of concrete between the reactor

144
00:20:08,780 --> 00:20:10,500
and personnel areas of the building.

145
00:20:10,500 --> 00:20:24,779
The HWCTR is designed to test fuel and other components under simulated power reactor conditions.

146
00:20:24,900 --> 00:20:29,779
A neutron flux of up to 10 to the 14th is available in the test core.

147
00:20:29,779 --> 00:20:42,819
The necessary reactivity is provided by 24 enriched uranium driver fuel assemblies

148
00:20:42,819 --> 00:20:51,960
positioned around the outer ring of the core A driver fuel assembly consists of a single 2 inch diameter

149
00:20:51,960 --> 00:20:57,079
10 foot long tube of 93% enriched uranium in a matrix of zirconium

150
00:20:57,079 --> 00:21:05,899
Each tube contains about 1 kg of uranium, which constitutes 9.7 weight percent of the alloy.

151
00:21:07,059 --> 00:21:13,240
Fabricated by the co-extrusion process, the fuel is clad with Zircaloy 2 or Zircaloy 4,

152
00:21:13,240 --> 00:21:15,919
with integrally extruded end plugs.

153
00:21:20,319 --> 00:21:26,179
Cruciform target leaves of stainless steel containing boron are positioned in the center of the fuel tube

154
00:21:26,180 --> 00:21:28,259
by a Zirkeloy support tube.

155
00:21:29,019 --> 00:21:35,259
This support and a ribbed outer housing tube define coolant flow on inner and outer surfaces of the fuel.

156
00:21:38,480 --> 00:21:42,620
The inner region of the core contains 12 test fuel positions

157
00:21:42,620 --> 00:21:46,759
in two staggered rings with six inner and six outer placings

158
00:21:46,759 --> 00:21:48,440
next to the driver assemblies.

159
00:21:50,340 --> 00:21:54,440
Inner positions will accept fuel elements of 3 1⁄2 inch diameter

160
00:21:54,440 --> 00:21:59,220
and outer positions can accept up to 5 inch diameter test assemblies.

161
00:22:00,660 --> 00:22:05,539
Test fuel elements will be in the form of natural uranium metal or oxide tubes

162
00:22:05,539 --> 00:22:09,340
or clusters of uranium oxide rods clad with zircaloy.

163
00:22:10,200 --> 00:22:12,660
Elements up to 10 feet in length can be accommodated.

164
00:22:15,539 --> 00:22:19,019
Twelve control rods and six safety rods are located

165
00:22:19,019 --> 00:22:22,380
between the driver and test fuel regions of the reactor core.

166
00:22:24,440 --> 00:22:28,519
A cluster of six additional control rods is located in the center.

167
00:22:30,279 --> 00:22:37,220
Six positions are provided within the test fuel core for in-pile instruments, such as axial flux monitors.

168
00:22:38,840 --> 00:22:44,640
The control rod drives and a lifting mechanism for the top head are attached to a movable platform,

169
00:22:45,120 --> 00:22:47,220
permitting clear entry to the reactor vessel.

170
00:22:54,440 --> 00:23:02,080
Loading and removal of fuel is accomplished by a shield transfer coffin, with the head

171
00:23:02,080 --> 00:23:03,940
and control rod mechanism removed.

172
00:23:03,940 --> 00:23:26,080
A telescoping gripper mechanism designed to attach to special adapter fittings

173
00:23:26,080 --> 00:23:29,700
permits handling of fuel assemblies and other reactor components.

174
00:23:33,940 --> 00:23:49,400
The transfer coffin carries spent fuel assemblies from the reactor to an underwater storage

175
00:23:49,400 --> 00:24:06,080
basin where they will remain until transfer to inspection facilities.

176
00:24:06,080 --> 00:24:11,080
In order to test fuel elements under a variety of physical conditions, the reactor vessel

177
00:24:11,080 --> 00:24:16,300
was designed with inlet and outlet ports to accommodate isolated coolant loops of the

178
00:24:16,299 --> 00:24:24,299
entrant type in the six outer test positions. Two such loops, both fabricated of stainless steel,

179
00:24:24,299 --> 00:24:30,059
were installed during construction of the reactor. One can be cooled with liquid D2O

180
00:24:30,059 --> 00:24:33,419
at pressures up to 500 psi above the core pressure.

181
00:24:35,819 --> 00:24:39,339
Coolant enters the lower connection of the isolated coolant loop tube

182
00:24:39,339 --> 00:24:45,619
and flows downward through the annulus between the outer pressure tube and the

183
00:24:45,619 --> 00:24:50,559
inner housing tube. It is then forced upward past the fuel assembly and

184
00:24:50,559 --> 00:25:00,279
emerges at the upper connection. Coolant is then circulated through the shell

185
00:25:00,279 --> 00:25:07,699
side of a D2O H2O heat exchanger. The cooled D2O is then circulated through a

186
00:25:07,700 --> 00:25:15,019
surge tank and back through two centrifugal pumps to the loop tube. The

187
00:25:15,019 --> 00:25:21,920
loop is pressurized with helium at the surge tank. Rupture discs between this

188
00:25:21,920 --> 00:25:26,299
gas line and the reactor pressurizing gas system provide for pressure relief

189
00:25:26,299 --> 00:25:34,740
of excessive loop pressure. The other installed loop will permit tests at

190
00:25:34,740 --> 00:25:40,460
boiling reactor conditions. The loop tube for the boiling loop is almost identical to that of the

191
00:25:40,460 --> 00:25:46,140
pressurized water loop. The cooling conditions of this loop can be adjusted to provide operating

192
00:25:46,140 --> 00:25:52,839
conditions over a range of interest to boiling D2O reactors. Steam qualities from 10 to 30 percent

193
00:25:52,839 --> 00:26:00,420
can be obtained at the outlet of the loop tube. The resulting liquid vapor mixture is condensed

194
00:26:00,420 --> 00:26:08,200
by liquid D2O in a steam quencher and is then circulated to the pressurizing surge tank.

195
00:26:09,560 --> 00:26:13,720
Pressure in this tank is provided by the reactor pressurizing gas system.

196
00:26:14,740 --> 00:26:21,240
The heated D2O is circulated back to the loop tube by two centrifugal pumps in series.

197
00:26:21,240 --> 00:26:30,680
cooling is provided by a bypass of the pump discharge which goes through a cooler similar

198
00:26:30,680 --> 00:26:38,000
to the liquid loop cooler the cooled d2o is then used to quench the vapor from the outlet of the

199
00:26:38,000 --> 00:26:44,500
loop tube control is guided by the temperature differential across the steam quencher the outlet

200
00:26:44,500 --> 00:26:50,460
steam quality is adjusted by the bypass rate through the cooler purification of the heavy

201
00:26:50,460 --> 00:26:55,100
water in the isolated coolant loops is accomplished in a common system. High

202
00:26:55,100 --> 00:26:59,819
pressure makeup pumps feed separate head tanks for each loop and inject clean

203
00:26:59,819 --> 00:27:08,059
heavy water into the systems. The two coolant loops of the main circulating

204
00:27:08,059 --> 00:27:17,220
system including the steam generators are fabricated of carbon steel. D20 at

205
00:27:17,220 --> 00:27:22,420
At about 420 degrees Fahrenheit, enters the reactor vessel through two inlet nozzles.

206
00:27:24,360 --> 00:27:29,880
It then circulates downward in a forced flow through each fuel tube, cooling the fuel.

207
00:27:34,000 --> 00:27:37,380
The reactor system is pressurized with helium gas.

208
00:27:39,100 --> 00:27:45,920
Heavy water leaves the reactor at the core section and is circulated by two 4,800 GPM high pressure pumps.

209
00:27:47,220 --> 00:27:56,220
A continuous purge of moderator from the reactor through filters and deionizers maintains the heavy water purity.

210
00:27:56,519 --> 00:28:06,240
A pH of greater than 10 required to protect the carbon steel piping and components from corrosion is provided by addition of lithium hydroxide to the system.

211
00:28:07,299 --> 00:28:17,200
Heat generated in the reactor core is dissipated through two large steam generators, each extracting approximately 30 megawatts of reactor heat at rated conditions.

212
00:28:17,220 --> 00:28:28,380
light water is supplied to the steam generators from a system of wells in the area outlet steam

213
00:28:28,380 --> 00:28:35,579
is used to drive the feed water pumps for these units the saturated light water steam equivalent

214
00:28:35,579 --> 00:28:46,539
to about 60 megawatts is vented to the atmosphere through a steam muffler all

215
00:28:46,539 --> 00:28:51,039
All reactor control operations are conducted from the adjoining control building, which

216
00:28:51,039 --> 00:29:01,680
houses the operating console, controls, and instruments for the operation of the reactor.

217
00:29:01,680 --> 00:29:06,539
In addition to control rods, control valves permit adjustment of the back pressure on

218
00:29:06,539 --> 00:29:11,639
the steam generators and through the action of a strong negative temperature coefficient

219
00:29:11,640 --> 00:29:16,700
of reactivity, adjusts the reactor power at almost constant moderator temperature.

220
00:29:17,759 --> 00:29:21,360
Inherent stability and ease of control characterize the design.

221
00:29:27,340 --> 00:29:32,380
The health physics building, also located near the containment building, is equipped

222
00:29:32,380 --> 00:29:37,040
with the necessary protective articles which all operating personnel use before entering

223
00:29:37,040 --> 00:29:38,080
the containment building.

224
00:29:41,640 --> 00:29:58,480
A 10-foot diameter air lock on the ground level permits access during operation.

225
00:29:58,480 --> 00:30:05,540
The major goal of the HWCTR is the development of inexpensive, natural uranium fuel elements

226
00:30:05,540 --> 00:30:10,400
which will withstand the long irradiation at high temperatures required for full-scale

227
00:30:10,400 --> 00:30:11,360
power reactors.

228
00:30:13,560 --> 00:30:19,180
Operation of the HWCTR will provide answers to many of the technical problems which must

229
00:30:19,180 --> 00:30:23,560
be resolved in development of the heavy water moderated natural uranium concept.

230
00:30:40,400 --> 00:30:53,860
The knowledge and experience gained from operation of the HWCTR

231
00:30:53,860 --> 00:30:59,360
can thus contribute immeasurably to the accomplishments of the power development efforts

232
00:30:59,360 --> 00:31:02,759
of the United States Atomic Energy Commission.
